[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Hausdorff dimension is defined for any subset of a metric space. For subsets[0m[38;2;124;124;124mof $\mathbb{R}^n$, the Hausdorff dimension is always at most $n$. This is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfundamental result.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me recall the key facts:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m1. For any subset $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, the Hausdorff dimension $\dim_H(A)[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\leq n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m2. This follows because the $n$-dimensional Hausdorff measure $H^n$ of any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbounded subset of $\mathbb{R}^n$ is finite (proportional to Lebesgue measure), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mand for $s > n$, the $s$-dimensional Hausdorff measure of any subset of $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{R}^n$ is 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me prove this properly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Hausdorff measure $H^s(A)$ for $s > n$ where $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider covering $A$ with balls of radius $r_i \leq \delta$. Each ball of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mradius $r_i$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$ is contained in a cube of side $2r_i$. The $n$-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional volume of a ball of radius $r$ is $c_n r^n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $s > n$: We can cover $\mathbb{R}^n$ (or any bounded subset) with cubes of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mside $\delta$. A bounded region of volume $V$ can be covered by roughly $V/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\delta^n$ cubes of side $\delta$. Each cube has diameter $\sqrt{n}\delta$, so [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontributes $(\sqrt{n}\delta)^s$ to the sum. Total: $\frac{V}{\delta^n} \cdot [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(\sqrt{n}\delta)^s = V \cdot n^{s/2} \cdot \delta^{s-n} \to 0$ as $\delta \to 0$[0m[38;2;124;124;124msince $s > n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $H^s(A) = 0$ for all $s > n$ and all $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ (at least for[0m[38;2;124;124;124mbounded $A$; for unbounded $A$, write as countable union of bounded pieces).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore $\dim_H(A) \leq n$ for all $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn particular, for $n = 2$, no subset of the 2D plane can have Hausdorff [0m
